orter proofs for some well-known theorems on set-intersections like Frankl-Wilson and Snevily with modular constraints, and some of the more recent set-intersection results. We also improve Snevily with modular constraints in some special cases. Finally, using Snevily's theorem with some combinatorial lemmas, we give new bounds on some generalizations of the reverse odd-town problem.\n\n  * Difference sets and tri-weight linear codes from trinomials over binary fields\n\nOmran Ahmadi, Masoud Shafaeiabr\n\nAug 24 2021 math.CO arXiv:2108.10042v1\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) The Cyclic Flats of a $q$-Matroid (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/359757081_The_Cyclic_Flats_of_a_q-Matroid)\nciteturn5search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Lemma 2.14, combined with Theorem 2.12, hence provides the following characterization ... In Example 2.17, we provides a speciﬁc counterexample. ... Now, we can apply the reconstruction result from the above discussion to obtain all the ﬂats ... ﬂats of classical polymatroids, we considered the question of generalizing the cryptomorphism ... minimal codes in the rank metric. arXiv preprint arXiv:2106.12465, 2021. ... number theory (London, 1993). ... The direct sum of q-matroids. arXiv preprint arXiv:2109.13637,\n\nmatroid (M|A)∗does not contain any loop.\n\nProof. If E is cyclic then it is the maximal element in the lattice of cyclic spaces of M. In\n\nparticular, E⊥= 0 is a ﬂat in the dual matroid M∗and this means that it is the minimal\n\nelement in the lattice of ﬂats of M∗. Hence, there are no loops in M∗. The second statement\n\nfollows immediately from Deﬁnition 1.7.\n\n6\n\nLemma 2.14. [7, Proposition 86] The ﬂats of the q-matroid M= (E, r) are the orthogonal\n\nspaces of the open spaces of the dual q-matroid M∗.\n\nLemma 2.14, combined with Theorem 2.12, hence provides the following characterization\n\nof cyclic spaces.\n\nCorollary 2.15. A≤E is cyclic i